window.docContents[218].resolve({"/Hoare2/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare2___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-II--Decorated-Programs--From-Decorated-Programs-to-Formal-Proofs":{"contents":"From an informal proof in the form of a decorated program, it is\n\"easy in principle\" to read off a formal proof using the Lean\ntheorems corresponding to the Hoare Logic rules, but these proofs\ncan be a bit long and fiddly.\n\n\n\n\n\nFor example...\n\ndef reduceToZero : Com :=\n  imp {\n    while (X ≠ 0) {\n      X := X - 1\n    }\n  }\n\n\n\n\ntheorem reduce_to_zero_correct' :\n    {{ True }} reduceToZero {{ X = 0 }} := by\n  -- First put the postcondition into the form expected by\n  -- the while rule.\n  apply hoare_consequence_post\n  -- We use the fact that after the `while` loop,\n  -- the loops condition must not hold.\n  · apply hoare_while\n    -- Trivial postcondition: we just need to prove `True`\n    · apply hoare_post_true; simp\n    -- Our precondition that `X ≠ 0` cannot hold is\n    -- exactly what we need to prove `X = 0`.\n  · simp\n    intros _ h; assumption\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA little more tactic fanciness for\nhelping deal with the boring parts of the process of proving\nassertions:\n\nExplain any other bits of it?\n\nmacro \"verify_assertion\" : tactic =>\n  `(tactic| assertion_auto)\n\n\n\n\nThis makes it pretty easy to verify reduce_to_zero:\n\ntheorem reduce_to_zero_correct''' :\n    {{ True }} reduceToZero {{ X = 0 }} := by\n  apply hoare_consequence_post\n  apply hoare_while\n  all_goals verify_assertion\n\n\n\n\nThis example shows that it is conceptually straightforward to read\noff the main elements of a formal proof from a decorated program.\nIndeed, the process is so straightforward that it can be\nautomated, as we will see next.\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Hoare2: Hoare Logic, Part II\u0009Decorated Programs","header":"6.1.5. From Decorated Programs to Formal Proofs","id":"/Hoare2/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare2___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-II--Decorated-Programs--From-Decorated-Programs-to-Formal-Proofs"}});